## Proof

Let $p(x) = ax^2 + bx + c$ where $a, b, c > 0$ and $a + b + c = 1$.

Let $x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n$ be any positive real numbers with $\prod_{i=1}^n x_i = 1$. We must show
$$\prod_{i=1}^n p(x_i) \ge 1.$$

**Key step — a pointwise lower bound.** Apply the weighted AM–GM inequality to the three positive quantities $x^2$, $x$, and $1$, using the weights $a$, $b$, $c$ (which are positive and sum to $1$):

$$a \cdot x^2 + b \cdot x + c \cdot 1 \;\ge\; (x^2)^{a}\,(x)^{b}\,(1)^{c}.$$

The left-hand side is exactly $p(x)$. The right-hand side simplifies to

$$(x^2)^a \cdot x^b \cdot 1^c = x^{2a} \cdot x^b = x^{2a+b}.$$

Thus for every $x > 0$,
$$p(x) \ge x^{2a+b}. \tag{$\ast$}$$

**Product step.** Applying $(\ast)$ to each $x_i$ and multiplying,

$$\prod_{i=1}^n p(x_i) \;\ge\; \prod_{i=1}^n x_i^{\,2a+b} \;=\; \left(\prod_{i=1}^n x_i\right)^{2a+b}.$$

Since $\prod_{i=1}^n x_i = 1$ and $2a+b$ is a fixed real number,

$$\left(\prod_{i=1}^n x_i\right)^{2a+b} = 1^{2a+b} = 1.$$

Therefore
$$\prod_{i=1}^n p(x_i) \ge 1,$$

as required. $\blacksquare$

